  "description": "A Wednesday normalization of the project names the fixture fire-rating interpretation. It ends Door 101's fire-rating story and fulfils Door 102's requirement of \"90 min\" by A-602's \"1.5 h\". On Thursday A-601 shows Door 103 as \"90 min\" and the specification \"1.5 h\", and A-601 changes Door 104 from \"1.5 h\" to \"90 min\". Frames naming no interpretation use the one in force: the Door 103 pair is matched by the normalization, Door 104 is unchanged, Door 102 is fulfilled and nothing remains. Frames requesting literal comparison compute every comparison literally at every instant: the Door 103 pair is a conflict, Door 104 changed, and Door 102's property outstanding, computed with no act, in the expectation, open work and what remains reads. Door 101's story keeps its standing and ending under a literal frame, which reports only that its sides do not match literally; on Tuesday, when the story's own interpretation was literal, no match is reported. The resolved frame reports the request as \"literal\", with no normalization, and every other interpretation member reports literal comparison as null. No recorded act changes.",
  "note": "4.6.1: `interpretation` is \"Either a named application interpretation, applied to every comparison of the read at every instant (5.5), or `\\\"literal\\\"`, which requests literal comparison (5.5.1) for every comparison of the read at every instant, whatever normalizations apply (7.3.8). ... The resolved frame returns a named interpretation, or a request for literal comparison, as given.\" 7.6.14: \"A frame that names another interpretation, or requests literal comparison (4.6.1), evaluates the expectation under that one at every instant\", and \"A story is always read under its own interpretation (7.2.4): a frame interpretation never changes how its standing is read, and the read reports whether the story's sides match under the frame's (9.5.1).\" README section 5, Frame: \"Only the resolved frame reports such a request as `\\\"literal\\\"`; every other `interpretation` member of a result, and of a recorded `@frame` (5.5.5), reports literal comparison as `null`\", and `normalization` \"is absent when the frame names an interpretation or requests literal comparison\".",
